#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124345 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124536 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/id #uuid "c262f28d-bfd0-5c78-bce7-d2ae314052e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5510" :question/problem "Given that ![](images/2e/2e4a7f95853bbfc023f9f4be8278063ed945889e84fd6b2bc2100a22dbd90994.svg) and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(7-\\lambda )+54 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}+A-2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}+A-2I) & =O \\\\ A+I-2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{1}{2}I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ $q=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124346 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124537 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/id #uuid "8e6d3017-d05d-5a2d-8043-c827e612476e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5511" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\textrm{tr}(A)=-4$ and $\\textrm{det}(A)=-18.$ If $A^2 = xA + yI$ for constants $x$ and $y,$ then find the value of $x+y.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2-\\textrm{tr}(A)\\lambda+\\textrm{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-4A+18I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x+y = -4+18 = 14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124347 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124538 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "ce724b3b-f440-5c1f-86a6-c50a54931233" :question/math-academy-id "e-5512" :question/problem "Given that ![](images/bd/bdf92550ba4b8fa58da146c944a99a57e35e13e4885c3a2309ab291a4a4177e4.svg) and $f(x) = x^{12} -x^{10} +2x-1,$ find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+15 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\:\\:x^{10}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$x^2-1\\:$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{12} \\, - \\, x^{10}  +  2x  -  1 {\\phantom{|}} }$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$x^{12}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$x^{10}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$2x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{10}({x}^{2}-1)+(2x-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x) = x^{10}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(2A-I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(2A-I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(2A-I) \\\\  & =2A-I \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124361 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124552 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "95b44b17-b796-51eb-93dd-4ee4b2e5c3f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5894" :question/problem "Given that ![](images/25/25c8dd8e95095754a68305847645f68c6e872e9049f5062177bbac4d2fcd1164.svg) find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda),$ i.e., the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2 = \\textrm{tr}(A)= 9+(-6)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124552 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07145787-a560-513d-aa3e-4cd4fbfc2c54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269333} {:answer/id #uuid "2e70d757-8dbc-5b1e-8d56-1c0b91cd3cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269687} {:answer/id #uuid "581ab64f-1b42-5217-847e-d9a9da445be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270047} {:answer/id #uuid "6fad9401-cd4e-5fd3-a325-22469ab1b06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270257} {:answer/id #uuid "d070a215-faf3-5d37-8c31-e4cc146c8ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "581ab64f-1b42-5217-847e-d9a9da445be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270047} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ccf049c-4adf-57b0-9a8a-d172a8a44396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f609dabc-35ae-55a4-ac90-dc75812d3aa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140519" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}=\\text{tr}(A)=4+1=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124537 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40aafa01-2db6-53ab-a1f6-d8532b623f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3A+4I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269855} {:answer/id #uuid "426f4bf5-761e-527d-95fd-f664b550420f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3A-2I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269867} {:answer/id #uuid "675b00bb-5f70-55fc-a677-066a1ea50901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A+7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270190} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1e6e78-3c17-5f66-9896-ef19fb9f7058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3A+7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270248} {:answer/id #uuid "738adfe9-b583-59a1-88dc-afcff4eb5173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8A+7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e1e6e78-3c17-5f66-9896-ef19fb9f7058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3A+7I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270248} :answer-field/id #uuid "790291ba-d5a0-5f22-bf0f-d016dbbcdf5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b78a275d-78f5-56e4-bf71-ab14cdbf1b29" :question/math-academy-id "q-140521" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\text{tr}(A)=3$ and $\\text{det}(A)=-7.$ Then, ${A}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =3A-(-7)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =3A+7I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124536 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a94ae5e-84f5-5fe7-9219-3b799a5dab21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}I+\\frac{1}{3}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269801} {:answer/id #uuid "62ae8d57-cee3-56ab-b4cf-5f863ad27aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270139} {:answer/id #uuid "7563f626-e047-55a1-8e63-8641f45069f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I-\\frac{1}{6}A+\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270306} {:answer/id #uuid "ad282852-264a-521a-92c0-2b9671da7fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A+\\frac{1}{12}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270812} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f15110-7592-5194-9248-7f09143aaa7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24I+\\frac{1}{12}A+\\frac{1}{6}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62ae8d57-cee3-56ab-b4cf-5f863ad27aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270139} :answer-field/id #uuid "1747bf63-e28c-57b2-a5d7-9c5c047b1742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b990092f-3b4e-5607-8795-8e6af5838a33" :question/math-academy-id "q-140531" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-2) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda +24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}-2{A}^{2}-11A+24I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}-2{A}^{2}-11A+24I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-2A-11I+24{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 24{A}^{-1} & =11I+2A-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{11}{24}I+\\frac{1}{12}A-\\frac{1}{24}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124536 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65f78fe5-8a0b-5c8c-9ba2-f234eec8b977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373780} {:answer/id #uuid "51d79449-387a-514d-b3bd-9b95e771984f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373781} {:answer/id #uuid "22be24f4-9f88-5ef3-8ae7-2e85d07ed518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373782} {:answer/id #uuid "84d983e6-6863-5892-b6cf-e9c5d7dfb428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373783} {:answer/id #uuid "de911146-38f6-5073-bdea-99b438169ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65f78fe5-8a0b-5c8c-9ba2-f234eec8b977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373780} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fff8c60-4f3f-55de-8054-ba67d1fcd863" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6944e235-260f-5526-a1a0-03870ed7a8fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-140534" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )+12 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +22 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-7A+22I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-7A+22I) & =O \\\\ A-7I+22{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{22}I-\\frac{1}{22}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{7}{22},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{22},$ and \n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{7}{22}+(-\\frac{1}{22})=\\frac{3}{11}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124538 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025a5c1a-4f6e-5e42-8dce-96a2fd08178a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -42 \\\\ 14 & -31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269289} {:answer/id #uuid "23310913-8cd2-557c-be76-7e2691fe0ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}31 & 14 \\\\ 42 & -25\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269575} {:answer/id #uuid "9142a6e7-f5cb-5d0d-bcaf-2c7c6f384044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 14 \\\\ -42 & 30\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270533} {:answer/id #uuid "efb97e7a-5224-5bc1-9035-2d0d54445cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 12 \\\\ -24 & -31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271376} {:answer/id #uuid "f7416ab1-633c-5a86-bade-cffdf59d2d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7416ab1-633c-5a86-bade-cffdf59d2d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271438} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d72f403-dcf6-5b06-9505-8ae246f18a3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af2db92b-ede0-52d7-a7d4-1043a63d9db0" :question/math-academy-id "q-140602" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{16}-4{x}^{14}+7x-3\\,$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )+12 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,{x}^{14}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{2}-4\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{16}\\,-\\,4{x}^{14}\\,+\\,7x\\,-\\,3\\phantom{|}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{16}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$4{x}^{14}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$7x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(7x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{14}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(7A-3I) \\\\  & =7A-3I \\\\  & =7\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -28\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 14 \\\\ -42 & -31\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124536 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0894f3ea-f301-59cb-8cc0-94e15917c23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3I-9A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544109} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf1ffa6-1400-563e-9431-a2cb4ab16e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9I+A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544286} {:answer/id #uuid "906cefe1-167f-55f1-a726-e75cd7b1f666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9I-9A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544404} {:answer/id #uuid "9be942a4-212d-50e7-ad1b-4d20819d8e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9I+3A-{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544423} {:answer/id #uuid "ca77d26f-42a8-5ca7-858c-4a827ebda512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3I+9A-{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "906cefe1-167f-55f1-a726-e75cd7b1f666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9I-9A+{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544404} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3364b42-0269-5bbd-853e-2a760baddc1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1aab1c27-1f53-564d-b691-068d98d1a09c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37780" :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 5 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )^{2}-15) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-9{\\lambda }^{2}+9\\lambda -1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}-9{A}^{2}+9A-I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}-9{A}^{2}+9A-I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-9A+9I-{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =9I-9A+{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124538 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "834aa185-6012-5fad-9cec-bfae8e36cc07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392142} {:answer/id #uuid "ba614c81-25e5-56b2-a980-247e1c339a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392143} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8f194b-9c47-54bc-9f21-95b6a53aec45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392144} {:answer/id #uuid "30a72e6d-1f6b-54c6-a30e-be3b5a7ecb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392145} {:answer/id #uuid "918cb8aa-dfd0-58f5-94c4-133741fe2164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30a72e6d-1f6b-54c6-a30e-be3b5a7ecb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392145} :answer-field/id #uuid "8edebb12-abdf-545c-97fb-264d8989834b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9892d6a4-00e5-53a4-a283-14b8510646e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-37835" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $\\,f(x)={x}^{22}-{x}^{20}+3x-2\\,$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+8 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,{x}^{20}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{2}-1\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{22}\\,-\\,{x}^{20}\\,+\\,3x\\,-\\,2\\phantom{|}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{22}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{20}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$3x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(3x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{20}.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(3A-2I) \\\\  & =3A-2I \\\\  & =3\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124538 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d95a80-3f16-57c4-aa0e-d7a0b263509f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 8 \\\\ -24 & -16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392149} {:answer/id #uuid "7ade6fcc-fefb-5c26-9596-732bb7e6456e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -24 \\\\ 8 & -16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392150} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8f35b7-246a-5615-af02-78cc8b431c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 16 \\\\ -48 & -31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392151} {:answer/id #uuid "bf93252a-5145-5f3e-a1e5-9eed34f606e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -25 \\\\ 18 & -42\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392152} {:answer/id #uuid "46d0fa8b-a3e4-5c81-b011-49838b661fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}40 & 24 \\\\ -70 & -44\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac8f35b7-246a-5615-af02-78cc8b431c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 16 \\\\ -48 & -31\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392151} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a7442f8-2e08-50f7-921a-87fb338cdbdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df379c8f-bbed-5154-8e34-f7aedf45ba74" :question/math-academy-id "q-37844" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 8 \\\\ -24 & -14\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $f(x)={x}^{12}-4{x}^{10}+{x}^{3}-2x-3$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}14-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -24 & -14-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(14-\\lambda )(-14-\\lambda )+192 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\,\\,{x}^{10}$ | $+$ | $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}-4\\,$ | $\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{12}-4{x}^{10}+{x}^{3}-2x-3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | ${x}^{12}$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,-$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  | ${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ | $-\\,\\,$ | $\\,\\,2x$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3$ |\n|  |  |  |  | ${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ | $-\\,\\,$ | $\\,\\,4x$ |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  | $\\,\\,2x$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(2x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{10}+x.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(2A-3I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(2A-3I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(2A-3I) \\\\  & =2A-3I \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 8 \\\\ -24 & -14\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 & 16 \\\\ -48 & -28\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}25 & 16 \\\\ -48 & -31\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124537 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eeaeba1-c3f6-5a7d-a932-2a93d587aa08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8A-12I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324111} {:answer/id #uuid "93630f9d-78cf-5ded-84e9-aa19f6f346a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8A-12I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325225} {:answer/id #uuid "d767c0ea-0291-536a-9373-acea6eed3f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8A+12I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325844} {:answer/id #uuid "e13638e9-fd7d-54e3-b7c7-c72968ee0f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12A-8I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325943} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d1e6eb-8e9b-5cb4-b09a-3fd437a73eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12A+8I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93630f9d-78cf-5ded-84e9-aa19f6f346a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8A-12I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325225} :answer-field/id #uuid "d94788c2-43f9-5632-900c-78fd51b91761" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56a1bd4c-16fb-5242-9ca4-9449cd0cb6e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-38702" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\text{tr}(A)=-8$ and $\\text{det}(A)=12.$ Then, ${A}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-8A-12I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124552 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "464c36cd-2fe4-5b44-bd24-1e13852890a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324537} {:answer/id #uuid "84de5614-afa3-5de8-9439-cccd0d1813cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325092} {:answer/id #uuid "a57ba301-c95c-59f2-ab39-2ee46770b140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325377} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c9fc74-c661-5a47-b72d-fc925632d9f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325505} {:answer/id #uuid "c309fd2a-88a7-5125-93ab-397e12924fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84de5614-afa3-5de8-9439-cccd0d1813cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325092} :answer-field/id #uuid "012c4b30-7193-5554-b48c-3161a2989269" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43c4e341-dfa0-5113-9166-43b962d6eac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-38703" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} & -2\\end{bmatrix},$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{5} & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{5} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124538 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee48dc36-244e-5623-8162-9e5def5de475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -22 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392227} {:answer/id #uuid "4e10d15f-edfd-50e4-802f-39336ec76684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392228} {:answer/id #uuid "7b98b20b-4948-5630-912d-d10854621cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392229} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ce0307-f7b7-5df0-93a8-a3b0f9edcea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -22 \\\\ 18 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392230} {:answer/id #uuid "52829169-8b9a-5477-a7ca-27875b4c0680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-22 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b98b20b-4948-5630-912d-d10854621cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392229} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2234961-7a5f-5c89-bdd3-f09c2cf9ab03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c6e1cb3-d3e5-5ac7-9b61-9f6c0c45ae57" :question/math-academy-id "q-38728" :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $f(x)={x}^{15}-3{x}^{13}+{x}^{3}-5x+6$, find $f(A).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}6-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(6-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )+33 \\\\  & ={\\lambda }^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,{x}^{13}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{2}-3\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{15}-3{x}^{13}+{x}^{3}-5x+6\\phantom{|}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{15}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{13}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,5x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,3x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,2x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =q(x)p(x)+r(x) \\\\  & =q(x)p(x)+(-2x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)={x}^{13}+x.$ Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(A)=q(A)p(A)+(-2A+6I).\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =q(A)p(A)+(-2A+6I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(-2A+6I) \\\\  & =-2A+6I \\\\  & =-2\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -22 \\\\ 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124536 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17bf09fd-3fd5-5fa9-bd7c-9bb8300133ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{3}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324178} {:answer/id #uuid "2196ad3b-dc18-575d-9ac5-f3e82f2cf1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324248} {:answer/id #uuid "ae47339b-004d-5c20-8d9d-8aab1f4f98f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2I-A-2{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325449} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9f773a-008b-571e-8538-a3402ba6e607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}I-\\frac{7}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325590} {:answer/id #uuid "d899671a-8147-5a25-9c47-966430b53806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9I-A-{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2196ad3b-dc18-575d-9ac5-f3e82f2cf1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324248} :answer-field/id #uuid "f00c9e90-925d-5d82-b4f9-f2d77c2b5637" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d383f46-698f-5924-8d8d-4466dfa087b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-40168" :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${A}^{-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )+3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{3}+{A}^{2}-7A+2I) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}+A-7I+2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ 2{A}^{-1} & =7I-A-{A}^{2} \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{2}I-\\frac{1}{2}A-\\frac{1}{2}{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124537 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bde7c58-3f7c-5801-a261-da5680905d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9A+5I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430396} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2122a1-0044-5550-a7b5-8c659f887082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5A+9I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430398} {:answer/id #uuid "8506e219-a01e-50d0-9da2-c59e5aa39951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9A-5I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430399} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd0659a-536b-5595-88dd-23740eda781b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5A-9I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430405} {:answer/id #uuid "f30920a8-926e-571d-b2ce-067087fbb78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5A-9I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfd0659a-536b-5595-88dd-23740eda781b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5A-9I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430405} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa304203-2130-5c26-be53-cf5953cd34e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad99eb94-2179-5889-bd75-4a31770a9b01" :question/math-academy-id "q-83394" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\text{tr}(A)=5$ and $\\text{det}(A)=9.$ Then, ${A}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =5A-9I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124537 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18acd11a-77da-5ce9-8385-47efc4e9103a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{A}^{3}-10{A}^{2}+12I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348653} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6a500f-c619-5a1e-a61c-09cf951d0fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{A}^{3}+10{A}^{2}-12I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348858} {:answer/id #uuid "43006f6d-8ba2-5499-b0f6-2f7860cc23f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{A}^{3}-{A}^{2}-12I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349055} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc71f59-c954-58d5-85c3-e00d5212b2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{3}+10{A}^{2}-2I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350240} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fcd47d-8817-5507-bf58-a9fbf0e3fa28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{3}+12{A}^{2}-10I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e6a500f-c619-5a1e-a61c-09cf951d0fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{A}^{3}+10{A}^{2}-12I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348858} :answer-field/id #uuid "c93e3f7e-ad65-5f30-86d0-9422353b3503" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfc38cc4-64d4-5447-b901-401955158f2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83397" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $4\\times 4$ matrix with the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}+2{\\lambda }^{3}-10{\\lambda }^{2}+12.$ Then, ${A}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{4}+2{A}^{3}-10{A}^{2}+12I & =O \\\\ {A}^{4} & =-2{A}^{3}+10{A}^{2}-12I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124537 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7719fc84-e762-543b-b613-975a49dbe0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14A-36I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349544} {:answer/id #uuid "84ca6bae-7c8a-5721-b681-106a1c7d43b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14A-36I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349659} {:answer/id #uuid "8ed2ee4a-8162-5cac-865c-a55ff918a2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36A-14I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349733} {:answer/id #uuid "bdff87b3-41f2-5792-92ea-62b9730ba659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14A+36I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350134} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6a820f-c0d3-527e-9575-15e72f9956a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36A+14I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdff87b3-41f2-5792-92ea-62b9730ba659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14A+36I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350134} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f723a74-0d5b-54ca-bd52-3d59889a4899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1eff7632-93b0-5245-bbd8-e6ffe12d224e" :question/math-academy-id "q-83398" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\text{tr}(A)=-14$ and $\\text{det}(A)=-36.$ Then, ${A}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-14A+36I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124552 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c45aed-2bbc-54d4-b4e4-b6e2dc638cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351634} {:answer/id #uuid "0a592ef9-dfb8-51e7-acb8-ad7c547f3518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351657} {:answer/id #uuid "362e0eff-6109-552b-9df5-68dc07e9d67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352071} {:answer/id #uuid "531691fc-36c2-5a15-8889-f0e39aa93c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352311} {:answer/id #uuid "66428265-caa7-5dda-bb91-ab3a19ee18ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07c45aed-2bbc-54d4-b4e4-b6e2dc638cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351634} :answer-field/id #uuid "5572322f-d1bd-5823-956f-c46a53ff8a5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81e19a7f-1aa2-5387-a0a2-afc232e386a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-85921" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{2})\\cdot 2-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =-7\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124552 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a38ceb7-78e0-536d-b6f4-5b0d0fb36963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352364} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c95411-022b-5df9-be2d-f24fb668a56a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352943} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a3ef71-3f78-5e0f-8cd1-ac73a5157b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353251} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f74d25-1c42-5e42-9c74-52425e6e436f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353305} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ef4f6b-04cb-5b34-8ba0-40661d358f31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2a3ef71-3f78-5e0f-8cd1-ac73a5157b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353251} :answer-field/id #uuid "3028fca6-be53-57d1-86aa-5aa3f7020ab2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d05c0df-0883-5373-84c3-aa2cb3f54a04" :question/math-academy-id "q-85923" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & -3\\end{bmatrix},$ find the product of the eigenvalues of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & ={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's Formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda ),$ i.e. the eigenvalues of $A,$ satisfy\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{tr}(A) \\\\ {\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} &=\\text{det}(A).\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the product of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the determinant of $A.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1}\\cdot {\\lambda }_{2} & =det(A) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-3)-\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-15-10 \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124536 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b7c0e75-3d49-5406-9041-ede1d5bfdf47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351962} {:answer/id #uuid "33bb1eb5-07b0-5c72-9e4a-a39b341c088c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352048} {:answer/id #uuid "6b960fbb-7fd4-564b-88d9-596a86197fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352519} {:answer/id #uuid "a471234f-1f9f-56ed-9886-d314b4205faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352971} {:answer/id #uuid "e3378a1b-e1c9-5e1d-b90b-a6157d273dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b7c0e75-3d49-5406-9041-ede1d5bfdf47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351962} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba728d29-f974-5f4e-bf33-9c1c26f466d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83ac805a-2248-5c38-94ec-8370eb490b39" :question/math-academy-id "q-85928" :question/problem "Given that $\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-7A+8I & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-7A+8I) & =O \\\\ A-7I+8{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =\\frac{7}{8}I-\\frac{1}{8}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{7}{8},$ $q=-\\frac{1}{8}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{7}{8}+(-\\frac{1}{8})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124538 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03dadc6e-37e2-586e-a762-b4874076a22b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20A+21I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351603} {:answer/id #uuid "341e8f60-2c2f-5139-b327-47e278c2e00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20A-21I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352054} {:answer/id #uuid "67808fb2-87cd-5157-a1e0-94315005596d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21A+20I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352488} {:answer/id #uuid "a32d7b40-8537-52c2-946a-2c71554d7a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21A-20I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352961} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3590d8-8a17-530d-9efd-1e18f0a866be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21A+20I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca3590d8-8a17-530d-9efd-1e18f0a866be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21A+20I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353325} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ced7d20-506d-5fe9-bbfe-309a56bbc614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62f9e5ba-0775-5b99-88ac-c3c5c53cda55" :question/math-academy-id "q-85931" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}-x-20,$ then ${A}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's divide the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}$ by characteristic polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}-x-20,$ using long division:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\,x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{2}-x-20\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"9\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{{x}^{3}\\phantom{|}\\,\\,}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{3}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{2}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$20x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{2}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$20x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">${x}^{2}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$-$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$21x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$+$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore, we can write ${x}^{3}$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =q(x)p(x)+(21x+20),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)=x+1.$  Replacing $x$ with $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{3}=q(A)p(A)+(21A+20I).\n$$\n\nNow, according to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =q(A)p(A)+(21A+20I) \\\\  & =q(A)\\cdot O+(21A+20I) \\\\  & =21A+20I.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
